-- Curated Keep TX Red article for 2026-08-07.
WITH seed AS (
  SELECT
    '2026-08-07-texas-tax-free-weekend-2026'::text AS slug,
    'Tax & Spending'::text AS category,
    'Texas Tax-Free Weekend Begins With Shoppers Expected to Save About $142.5 Million'::text AS title,
    'Texas’ annual sales-tax holiday runs through Sunday, covering many school supplies, clothes, shoes and backpacks priced below the state’s eligibility limits.'::text AS dek,
    'Houston Chronicle'::text AS source_name,
    'https://www.houstonchronicle.com/news/houston-texas/trending/article/sales-tax-holiday-22375111.php'::text AS source_url,
    '2026-08-07T12:12:00Z'::timestamptz AS published_at,
    23::int AS score,
    FALSE::boolean AS is_breaking,
    '{"updated":"2026-08-07","intro":["Texas’ annual back-to-school sales-tax holiday is underway, giving families a three-day window to buy many clothes, shoes, backpacks and school supplies without state and local sales tax. State officials estimate shoppers will save nearly $142.5 million this year, making the weekend one of the most visible tax breaks on the Texas calendar."],"sections":[{"heading":"How the weekend works","paragraphs":["The holiday began Friday and continues through midnight Sunday. Eligible purchases can be made in stores, online, by telephone or by mail, which means shoppers are not limited to traditional brick-and-mortar retailers. The exemption generally applies to qualifying school supplies, clothing, footwear and backpacks that meet the state’s price rules.","Texas normally layers state and local sales taxes on retail purchases, with the combined rate reaching 8.25% in many communities. Removing that tax can produce meaningful savings on a large family shopping list, although the exact benefit depends on what is purchased and whether each item qualifies. Shoppers should keep receipts in case a retailer mistakenly charges tax on an eligible product."]},{"heading":"The size of the savings","paragraphs":["Texas officials project almost $142.5 million in consumer savings during the 2026 holiday, roughly $10 million more than the prior year. The state says Texans have saved more than $2 billion through the program since it began in 1999. Those figures show why the weekend remains popular even as shopping habits have shifted toward earlier online purchases and year-round discounting.","For an individual household, the tax break is smaller than the statewide number makes it sound. A family spending $500 on fully eligible goods in an area with an 8.25% combined rate would avoid a little more than $40 in tax. The larger opportunity often comes from combining the tax exemption with retailer promotions, coupons and price matching."]},{"heading":"Back-to-school costs are still high","paragraphs":["The holiday arrives as many parents face a broad list of expenses that extends beyond pencils and jeans. Houston-area survey data cited by the Chronicle suggests families expect to spend substantially on back-to-school purchases, and technology can make up a major share of the bill. Laptops, tablets and other electronics are not broadly covered by the traditional sales-tax holiday.","That mismatch has renewed debate over whether the program reflects how students actually prepare for school in 2026. Families increasingly need devices, software, headphones, chargers and other technology alongside traditional supplies. Supporters of expanding the exemption argue that the tax code should catch up with modern classrooms, while critics question whether short tax holidays are the most efficient way to deliver relief."]},{"heading":"Who benefits most","paragraphs":["Households with several school-age children can save more because they are buying larger quantities of eligible goods. Families that can time purchases for the weekend also gain more than shoppers who had to buy earlier for camps, uniforms or school start dates. Retailers benefit from a predictable burst of traffic and often schedule promotions around the event.","At the same time, the holiday does not help every family equally. Lower-income households may not have enough cash available to concentrate purchases into one weekend, and some essential school-related costs do not qualify. That is one reason policy analysts often distinguish between the popularity of sales-tax holidays and their effectiveness as targeted economic assistance."]},{"heading":"What shoppers should watch for","paragraphs":["Consumers should verify that individual items are eligible rather than assuming an entire back-to-school order is tax-free. Mixed online carts can include both exempt and taxable products, and shipping or bundled pricing can affect how a transaction is treated. If tax is charged incorrectly, the state says customers can ask the seller for a refund.","Shoppers should also compare final prices rather than focusing only on the tax label. A retailer offering a lower price earlier in the summer can still beat a full-price tax-free purchase. The strongest savings strategy is to use the exemption on items that are already competitively priced instead of buying something simply because the tax has temporarily disappeared."]},{"heading":"Why the policy debate will continue","paragraphs":["The sales-tax holiday has survived for decades because it is simple to understand and arrives at a moment when families are already spending money. That political durability is likely to continue. The bigger question is whether lawmakers expand the eligible list or extend the length of the holiday to include more modern school expenses.","Proposals to add laptops or create a longer shopping window would increase consumer savings but also reduce state and local tax collections. Texas lawmakers will have to weigh those tradeoffs against broader tax-relief options. For this weekend, however, the practical message is straightforward: qualifying essentials can be purchased without sales tax through Sunday night.","Even after the weekend ends, the size and design of the exemption will remain a recurring Texas policy question. Supporters can point to immediate household savings during one of the most expensive shopping periods of the year, while critics can ask whether a short holiday is the most efficient way to provide tax relief and whether the list of eligible goods keeps pace with how students actually learn. The exclusion of many electronics is an obvious example: laptops and other technology can be central school expenses but generally sit outside the traditional clothing-and-supplies framework. Those arguments are likely to return whenever lawmakers revisit the sales-tax code."]},{"heading":"Source attribution","paragraphs":["This article is an independent Keep TX Red rewrite based on reporting from Houston Chronicle. The original report is linked below for verification."]}],"faq":[],"sources":[{"label":"Houston Chronicle — original report","url":"https://www.houstonchronicle.com/news/houston-texas/trending/article/sales-tax-holiday-22375111.php"}],"keyTakeaways":["Texas’ annual sales-tax holiday runs through Sunday, covering many school supplies, clothes, shoes and backpacks priced below the state’s eligibility limits."]}'::jsonb AS body_json
), prepared AS (
  SELECT seed.*,
    (SELECT string_agg(value, ' ') FROM jsonb_array_elements_text(
      jsonb_path_query_array(seed.body_json, '$.intro[*]') ||
      jsonb_path_query_array(seed.body_json, '$.sections[*].paragraphs[*]')
    )) AS body_text
  FROM seed
)
INSERT INTO public.daily_articles
  (slug, internal_url, is_ingested, category, title, dek, source_name, source_url,
   published_at, score, is_breaking, kind, body, body_json)
SELECT slug, '/news/' || slug, FALSE, category, title, dek, source_name, source_url,
       published_at, score, is_breaking, 'news', body_text, body_json
FROM prepared
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET
  internal_url=EXCLUDED.internal_url, is_ingested=EXCLUDED.is_ingested,
  category=EXCLUDED.category, title=EXCLUDED.title, dek=EXCLUDED.dek,
  source_name=EXCLUDED.source_name, source_url=EXCLUDED.source_url,
  published_at=EXCLUDED.published_at, score=EXCLUDED.score,
  is_breaking=EXCLUDED.is_breaking, kind=EXCLUDED.kind,
  body=EXCLUDED.body, body_json=EXCLUDED.body_json;
